From 767c0aa455587478a6682048b3ed6a4c1e9880c7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 16:19:10 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 157/174] fix: a grade file dropped into assets/cinecam
 registers itself, one file at a time
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Item 4b's step 5 failed: copying a third-party .cube into
assets/cinecam/luts/ produced no .cryasset, no Asset Browser entry and no
log line at all.

Root cause. The stock per-file watcher, CAssetGenerator, does reach our
extensions - the plugin loads before CAssetManager::Init, so cube/cdl/
cinegrade are in its listener list. It then throws the file away at
AssetGenerator.cpp:96-100, because CAssetType::IsValidAssetPath forbids a
dot in the asset base name (AssetType.cpp:382-388), and it discards the
reason it was given. The file was called
XT3_FLog_FGamut_to_ETERNA_BT.709_33grid_V.1.01.cube. Vendor LUT names
carry colour-space names, version numbers and spaces as a matter of
course, and CryStringUtils::IsValidFileName rejects the last of those
too.

Two more layers behind it. "Generate/Repair Metadata" could never have
helped: it runs one fixed job file, Tools/cryassets/rcjob_cryassets.xml,
whose sixteen-extension list contains none of ours - so the documented
fallback would have regenerated the whole asset directory and still not
registered the LUT. And the whole stock path is an rc.exe round trip per
file, which is a dependency we must not add.

Mechanism of the fix, entirely inside CinematicCameraEditor.dll and with
no Resource Compiler anywhere in it:

* all three asset types now return CanAutoRepairMetadata() == false. That
  takes their extensions out of CAssetGenerator's file-monitor list and
  out of its /assettypes= string (AssetGenerator.cpp:159-168), so the
  stock watcher never fires on them and two writers cannot race for the
  same sidecar with two different GUIDs. CScriptType and CXmlType already
  say the same thing;
* CineCamAssetRegistration.cpp registers an AssetManagerHelpers::
  CAsyncFileListener on assets/cinecam/ for the three extensions - which
  brings the FIFO queue and the "the file is still being written, retry
  in half a second" logic with it, and which accepts Created as well as
  Modified, unlike the stock watcher;
* the same file sweeps assets/cinecam/ once on
  CAssetManager::signalScanningCompleted, for files copied in while the
  editor was closed;
* three CAssetImporters, one per extension, make drag-and-drop onto the
  Asset Browser and File -> Import work at all: the drop handler gates on
  importers and never looks at asset types (AssetDropHandler.cpp:67-83).
  One importer per extension so the import dialog offers one type rather
  than asking whether a .cube might be a CDL;
* cinecam_RegisterAssets on the console re-runs the sweep by hand.

All four entry points share one writer: new CAsset + CEditableAsset::
WriteToFile - the engine's own sidecar writer, reached through the
exported CAssetImportContext::CreateEditableAsset - then MergeAssets, the
same two calls CAssetFileMonitor::LoadAsset makes.

Two invariants it never breaks: an existing .cryasset is never rewritten
(it carries the asset's GUID, and re-issuing that would break every
reference to it), and nothing outside assets/cinecam/ is touched on our
own initiative - the importer being the exception, because the user
pointed at the destination folder.

Names IsValidAssetPath rejects are deliberately accepted. That rule is a
creation-time rule for types RC compiles; nothing on the loading side
enforces it, so the sidecar loads, lists and picks correctly. The only
consequence is that the browser's Name column cuts the label at the first
dot, which is documented.

Nothing outside the editor plugin changed: no renderer, no engine plugin,
no EditorQt, no EditorCommon, no RC config. Docs: README's picker
paragraph, SceneReferredLook §2 and its troubleshooting table,
ConsoleReference gains cinecam_RegisterAssets.

